4iQZHMVAD2HQIZ1WBJC2cORqHRwjvcgYs2-xx9iu7AMQG1UlLho=","id":"rs_0cdcf22d3808a85f006a95bbd7218887d09fb602e6a1419ef0","summary":[{"text":"**Identifying the Sequence**\n\nThe user wants to explore the pattern in the sequence: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30. I see that it looks like these are double the triangular numbers, which are defined as T_n = n(n+1)/2. When I double these values, I indeed get the sequence: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30. So, the nth term is given by the formula a_n = n(n+1). This highlights that these numbers are pronic numbers, being products of consecutive integers.","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Deriving the Formula**\n\nI can express the nth term as a_n = 2 times the triangular number or as a_n = 2*C(n+1, 2), but the simplest form is a_n = n(n+1). The sequence shows constant second differences, confirming it's quadratic. The differences between terms are even numbers: 4, 6, 8, and 10, with differences increasing by 2. After solving the equations formed, I found a_n = n^2 + n, identifying it as pronic numbers—products of consecutive integers starting from 1.","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Finalizing the Answer**\n\nThe pattern I've identified consists of numbers in the form n(n+1). Therefore, I can express the nth term as a_n = n(n+1) or a_n = n^2 + n. These numbers are also twice the triangular numbers, since T_n = n(n+1)/2 leads to a_n = 2T_n. Notably, the first differences form an arithmetic progression: 4, 6, 8, 10. Given that the second difference is constant at 2, the general term simplifies to a quadratic equation: a_n = n^2 + n.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"The terms are the “pronic” (or oblong) numbers, i.e. products of two consecutive integers:\n\n   1·2 = 2, 2·3 = 6, 3·4 = 12, 4·5 = 20, 5·6 = 30, …\n\nHence if you start counting at n=1 the nth term is\n\n   aₙ = n·(n + 1)\n\nor equivalently\n\n   aₙ = n² + n.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_0cdcf22d3808a85f006a95bbf0c93087d0a6bcf30b13f4e166","role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message"}],"project_id":"f1e858a4-58e3-408f-983f-016760d7fa25","root_span_id":"0cc4a1016d9cfaeed42dd99357f1a116","scores":null,"span_attributes":{"name":"responses","type":"llm"},"span_id":"fbf2c2de8fdc290a","span_parents":["15d926ed2e259f58"],"tags":null},{"_async_scoring_state":null,"_pagination_key":"p07680251456632848384","_xact_id":"1000197783589884487","audit_data":[{"_xact_id":"1000197783589884487","audit_data":{"action":"upsert"},"metadata":{},"source":"api"}],"classifications":null,"comments":null,"context":{"span_origin":{"instrumentation":{"name":"braintrust-java"},"name":"braintrust.sdk.java","version":"0.3.22-ca5ce6d-DIRTY"}},"created":"2026-08-31T17:37:57.225Z","error":null,"expected":null,"facets":null,"id":"49baeacf4125b3ba","input":[{"content":"Look at this sequence: 2, 6, 12, 20, 30. What is the pattern and what would be the formula for the nth term?\n","role":"user"},{"content":[],"encrypted_content":"gAAAAABqlbv0R1eufJtJbpIkOfSDwwyF5lQNyx50eyNFEAQYx630DXHxgoY9Jq3Tr-55UnSd6tmfLMfB-J6kUQ8cC4ou18k1bNH5wWN2tr8R6qRHS5_7b9ars4VkbJ9VOo4vEghADLo3XSZLVDqAxJPv17iLQXxfkTXgO-3SlFFYdFt-P8qQsW6eGGzQvZBSrN4E14vZPfPUleTkWwttsdlyA8Ma7Yio2rhmQOyflOhQfU2QVZ7s2JwvAPAbjF8_e5MHsD5jvhzvGWhHOKlNJrQ2Jd9tMQjkzWciwEzy1wjxP52x0m-in6cEHr2zhh_rKwHtOUSeC8DvqjeY76hOH4vLuuGe6GWxvQnhRZvu4MUdnPh0V9DiYiNwcaf2oawPKGQ81Ym3qwXRM2LC-qvYDJ2NHDkCNHZW7FSoJt8w-T7KhT-Bs-VaV4biMqs0sPHkJQ2Fs9yBSu8SqauxboASnx5ivXIsO4tI860xy4cW1B9-aWyqdFXSvi4QKYf7FuGU_aWbNr8-atpgj8Z_NITWh9z7XUXTgPHQ8I9wBoA4ThK1gicMnyG6HalA7lmeUeMhiSnfUMdOCAhp2_orJf4l0EZ3PMCxneIiFIXeoMIPq3YVQm6kswbEROUTFG3JlKchrTJ6Fed5sbtAGlN4vektll9l6U1iWgL8ZjAI995qSe6zcf0UhE75sR_KZNSS5W-jWFO1k-FzfrBouMzTYegfBmllfZ2DbA_iQz8chQoDQyb2-_9ALHs9Gq6F8ar5KY1jbnh1RV1jlRsXlAfjG9-4ibqyRHsHv7bMLe0M4SngpnEhpK-G-RCLk3L5ij4Jh3Vp_amD3o6LpLzcpANbLac-geQFzxUzImDO_Ior0CAGLyiMemXE0mBY5auEtsDny6SRKwZH98bluWH-U9PFB_nONLq07klJZuygho9BJ7Uc51jwXL279U74DjTaH0yfZhFckXfeIsEfOHRkJUVtXeiqUtKksgnzrZ-WHyg-2VLIvoLMEqcK1o